 1 big thing: AI&#x27;s free-will fight 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Top AI executives are increasingly pushing back against a common view inside Anthropic: that humanity should surrender decision-making power to superintelligent AI systems, sources told Axios' Madison Mills. 

 Some Silicon Valley insiders see that as a first step toward giving up our free will. Why it matters: The debate has picked up steam as top AI companies develop models many employees consider too powerful to ever release .

 The critics haven't named Anthropic, but their calls to keep humans in charge run counter to a view some in Silicon Valley associate with its CEO, Dario Amodei.

 Amodei has repeatedly said it would be preferable to turn over decision-making for "life-critical" cases to AI when systems far surpass human intelligence. He's also said AI should oversee research and potentially distribute economic resources. Flashback: A product vision slide from several years ago that was revealed during litigation described Anthropic's "vision for a better world" as a "utopia ruled by god in a box."

 🖼️ The big picture: The thinking for Amodei and others is that once AI can demonstrably cure diseases, safeguard humans from harming themselves or prevent disasters better than any person, refusing to hand over control becomes irrational — or even unethical.

 Proponents point to self-driving cars to make their case: Once statistical evidence points to autonomous vehicles being safer than human drivers, it is logical for humans to drive far less, if at all, they say. Inside Anthropic, a consensus has developed that humanity will — and in many cases should — safely hand over authority to superintelligent systems rather than fighting to retain control, according to people familiar with internal discussions. An Anthropic spokesperson said Amodei has advocated for human control of AI in public remarks and has developed the technology to ensure Claude doesn't undermine human oversight. Friction point: Two of the industry's most prominent executives say people should keep the final say, even if AI can outthink them.

 Meta's top AI leader, Alexandr Wang, wrote on X that "we should not be okay with a 'handover' of society to AI." OpenAI CEO Sam Altman said earlier this month: "I want people to have the agency over the future. ... I think it's really important that we advocate for this." Share this story ... Get Axios AI+ . 

 
 
