 3. 🎤 &quot;The Axios Show&quot;: Wes Moore on &quot;AI earthquake&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Mike Allen interviews Maryland Gov. Wes Moore (D) in the Maryland State House in Annapolis on Monday. Photo: JaMar Jones for Axios Maryland Gov. Wes Moore (D), a potential 2028 presidential contender, told me on "The Axios Show" that Washington is leaving Americans vulnerable to AI because of billionaire greed and clout.

 Moore, chair of the National Governors Association, said that after an " AI earthquake " helped propel Democrats to huge midterm gains, Congress must treat oversight of the technology as an "emergency" for the first 100 hours of Democratic control. Why it matters: Moore, 48, focused on AI early, with an executive order in 2024 . He has used his NGA leadership roles to work across the aisle on AI protections, arguing that Marylanders can't afford to wait for Washington and shouldn't have to wait for a disaster . 

 The governor's key points:

 On the pace of regulation: "We are watching a fast-moving technology that is being protected at a snail's pace." On why people hate AI: "Genesis 1 talks about how God gave humanity dominion over all land, air and sea. And that dominion was given to humanity, not to robots." On a bubble: "We are in a bubble because you cannot have that much influence by such a small group of companies." ▶️ Watch the interview . 

 
 
