 5. 🎯 Scoop: Midterm friendly fire 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photos: Andrew Harnik, Bill Clark/CQ-Roll Call via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A top executive at one of the Republican Party's most influential consulting firms was involved in a multimillion-dollar effort to defeat two GOP senators — Jon Husted of Ohio and Dan Sullivan of Alaska — in midterms, Axios' Alex Isenstadt has learned. 

 The firm, Axiom Strategies, is widely seen as the GOP's biggest consulting operation, with deep ties to Texas Sen. Ted Cruz and other prominent Republican figures. Why it matters: It's exceedingly rare — if not unprecedented — for an executive at a major political consulting firm to work for groups seeking to defeat senators from the firm's own party in a general election.

 🔎 Zoom in: Federal election records identify Thomas Datwyler, who was listed on Axiom's website as CEO of its AxCapital division, as treasurer and custodian of records for three super PACs that reported more than $9.9 million in spending that targeted Husted and Sullivan, who lost their reelection bids.

 Axiom founder Jeff Roe learned of Datwyler's involvement from a lawyer on Oct. 30 and demanded his resignation, according to a source with knowledge of the situation. Datwyler resigned that day, and Axiom says it will finish unwinding its relationship with him by Dec. 31. People familiar with his work said he sometimes accepted clients without scrutinizing their objectives. Roe contacted the White House to inform officials of what he had discovered, the source said. Keep reading . 

 
 
